#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9a9b6de-b0dd-5b38-a32b-378d2b1d86a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Difference of Sets Without a Venn Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44e307c6-002e-5e39-9462-8c2b1716b655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{6,12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238653} {:answer/id #uuid "5a0c7918-8746-5623-8404-e4d63b879f46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{1,16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238831} {:answer/id #uuid "5c38df76-1c73-51ed-b432-0053aa387801" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238861} {:answer/id #uuid "94095acb-889f-5411-938e-94c7032f9443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{6,10,12,14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239335} {:answer/id #uuid "e5b0a078-558e-53d8-88af-11b36981fb60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2,4,8,16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94095acb-889f-5411-938e-94c7032f9443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{6,10,12,14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239335} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3174f2e-e4fe-5b15-9ca8-8ba15281d2ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1c945ab-a73d-5e4c-9433-39a80d9da5a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-105217" :question/problem "If $A={1,2,4,8,16}$ and $B={2,4,6,8,10,12,14,16},$ then $B∖A=$" :question/worked-solution "The set $B∖A$ is the set of all elements belonging to $B$ but *not* $A.$ Note that the elements $2,4,8,16\\in A$ are also in $B,$ so we must remove them. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B∖A & =B-A \\\\  & ={2,4,6,8,10,12,14,16}-{1,2,4,8,16} \\\\  & ={6,10,12,14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119809 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a04b551f-3323-5b91-b01e-941912cc92be" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Lies on a Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "202fca4b-d4db-559c-86f9-155db3d13c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238333} {:answer/id #uuid "2b56c1db-87eb-5ec9-914d-30a522b26fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238442} {:answer/id #uuid "6182bb36-df22-5b55-8de2-18be6f4e6edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238911} {:answer/id #uuid "a846d2c7-f2b2-52b6-aa5d-db3effda179c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239517} {:answer/id #uuid "f503a4ab-d69a-5e93-a2e0-b67020545fa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6182bb36-df22-5b55-8de2-18be6f4e6edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238911} :answer-field/id #uuid "13f36fa9-d65b-5d54-8f7f-9bbf30419197" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "caedc8c2-ba3c-5e61-ad1f-0d5eafbb8652" :question/math-academy-id "q-105257" :question/problem "Determine if the vector $u$ lies on the plane $Π:r=sb+tc,$ $t,s\\in R$, where  $b=⟨1,-2,-1⟩$, $c=⟨-4,5,-1⟩$, and  $u=⟨1,1,4⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $u=sb+tc.$" :question/worked-solution "By definition, $u\\in Π$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc & =u \\\\ s[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s-4t=1 \\\\ -2s+5t=1 \\\\ -s-t=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s-4t=1 \\\\ t=-1 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nt=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s-4t & =1 \\\\ s-4(-1) & =1 \\\\ s+4 & =1 \\\\ s & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=-3,\\,t=-1,$ we conclude that $u$ does indeed lie on the plane $Π.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\nu=-3b-c.\n$$\n\nFinally, $s\\cdot t=(-3)\\cdot (-1)=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272056 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129132 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c28b8ae-71ef-5d26-895a-5d949fc5b164" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Components of the Real and Imaginary Parts of a Complex Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85da12d4-ccb2-5dce-93ac-5e138a668cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270429} {:answer/id #uuid "9fb9db6d-cb8c-529c-98b2-df8e6c79a815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270688} {:answer/id #uuid "a0c6ca61-603a-5e8a-89b1-f8e286912157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270695} {:answer/id #uuid "a38d5a79-23af-54f5-b33c-13fd728ca281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270726} {:answer/id #uuid "c1fce5ad-0b4c-5edd-8aa1-b1eaf719d57b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1fce5ad-0b4c-5edd-8aa1-b1eaf719d57b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270985} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d009f0a-0694-5fcc-8aef-3470ada89bbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "11dcfffe-2217-5164-89c5-20cf281ca601" :question/math-academy-id "q-140325" :question/problem "If $\\,\\text{Re}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\text{Im}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}e & f \\\\ g & h\\end{aligned}]\\,$ are the real and imaginary parts of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3-9\\text{i} & -7+\\text{i} \\\\ -5-2\\text{i} & -4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ then what is the value of $bc-ef?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-3-9\\text{i} & -7+\\text{i} \\\\ -5-2\\text{i} & -4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ A & =[\\begin{aligned}-3-9\\text{i} & -7+\\text{i} \\\\ -5-2\\text{i} & 0-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -7 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(A)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(A)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $b=-7,c=-5,e=-9$ and $f=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nbc-ef=(-7)\\cdot (-5)-(-9)\\cdot 1=44.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d44cf726-a4a6-5ac9-a63c-7464fe5566c5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given in Parametric or Vector Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "009411d3-d832-5609-913e-acebd7d7bf4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{5}=\\frac{z}{-6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336035} {:answer/id #uuid "092a7315-5896-5792-a85d-57b6c0dd114b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+4}{2}=\\frac{y+5}{-3}=\\frac{z-6}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336111} {:answer/id #uuid "462f0843-eb05-5ebd-9a15-d012956547f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-4}{2}=\\frac{y-5}{-3}=\\frac{z+6}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336684} {:answer/id #uuid "68221d0f-377e-5a6b-b460-b93c5443528c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y+3}{5}=\\frac{z-7}{-6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336961} {:answer/id #uuid "bca2c6fa-f347-5c3e-83d6-457baf506743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+2}{4}=\\frac{y-3}{5}=\\frac{z+7}{-6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68221d0f-377e-5a6b-b460-b93c5443528c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y+3}{5}=\\frac{z-7}{-6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336961} :answer-field/id #uuid "457d9519-cf87-50e2-920f-0c4455b746d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cadba95b-e6bb-5c0e-9d1a-07af961b4052" :question/math-academy-id "q-71020" :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the parametric equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2+4t \\\\ y=-3+5t \\\\ z=7-6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2+4t \\\\ -3+5t \\\\ 7-6t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ 5t \\\\ -6t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThis implies that the given line passes through the point $P(2,-3,7)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨4,5,-6⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y-(-3)}{5}=\\frac{z-7}{-6}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y+3}{5}=\\frac{z-7}{-6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147495 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4535b48e-a33f-5f62-95d3-a5c0991c1f6d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10267759-948c-5e21-b819-eea0a52b9c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360894} {:answer/id #uuid "21c0f117-930c-5f72-bd6c-e01d53d016cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360984} {:answer/id #uuid "8db2a840-a6b6-51d4-8f6c-92bd0dad5406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361466} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c1cb60-43c1-5cc0-a644-c5f45b9d687b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361644} {:answer/id #uuid "c000b11d-6918-5ea4-9dcf-dd56873b8480" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c000b11d-6918-5ea4-9dcf-dd56873b8480" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361695} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e224e7e-dfd1-5c42-9dff-859b4fa1ddef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1d8d74b-55cf-51de-803f-0cecc60a4fb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-98714" :question/problem "![](images/6b/6b9b15bd3c8cda84156d5461fb9f80f892a1dba26151cc37d381757f9f659fa1.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=-3$ is a node.\n- $y=2$ is a sink.\n- $y=2$ is a source." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/36/362ffe8871efa8dc597708b039fa766c3603777ba12460e84d9af1fc39800288.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-3$ is a node.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=2$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}]}
